From 655d7dfe13f378653eb31c25eedfa2532fd94dd6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Fri, 3 Apr 2026 13:50:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] fix MTK boot-black-screen: reset HWC power state directly

on MTK devices the bootloader leaves the display powered (HWC mode 2)
but the kernel's disable_unused clock framework kills the DSI clocks
before userspace starts.  during initializeDisplays(), SF's internal
power mode defaults to OFF, so setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(OFF)
early-returns without reaching HWC.  the subsequent ON request reaches
HWC, which sees current=2 and requested=2 and takes a no-op shortcut,
leaving the dead display hardware uninitialised.

the previous fix attempted to work around this by setting SF's internal
DisplayDevice state to ON before the OFF call, defeating the early
return.  however this desynchronised the mRefreshableDisplays counter:
the direct setPowerMode(ON) bypassed incRefreshableDisplays(), so the
subsequent OFF decremented the counter below zero.  the final ON only
brought it back to 0 instead of 1, preventing vsync dispatch from
being re-enabled (omitVsyncDispatching stayed true).

fix by sending OFF directly to HWC via getHwComposer().setPowerMode()
before the normal SF power-off path.  this resets HWC's internal state
from 2 to 0 without touching SF's state tracking or the refreshable
counter.  the SF OFF call still early-returns (harmless), and the
subsequent ON does a clean 0->2 transition with correct counter state.
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index eb481b1e1c..77fab5e5fb 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -6060,7 +6060,15 @@ void SurfaceFlinger::initializeDisplays() {
         ftl::FakeGuard guard(mStateLock);
 
         // In case of a restart, ensure all displays are off.
+        // on some vendor HALs (notably MTK) the bootloader leaves the
+        // display powered (HWC mode 2) but the kernel's disable_unused
+        // clock framework kills the DSI clocks before userspace starts.
+        // SF's internal state defaults to OFF, so the call below would
+        // early-return without reaching HWC.  send OFF directly to HWC
+        // first to reset its internal state, then let the normal ON
+        // loop do a proper full 0->2 power-on through DRM/KMS.
         for (const auto& [id, display] : mPhysicalDisplays) {
+            getHwComposer().setPowerMode(id, hal::PowerMode::OFF);
             setPhysicalDisplayPowerMode(getDisplayDeviceLocked(id), hal::PowerMode::OFF);
         }
 
